=== Mergers===

*'''Kami merger'''
On [[April 1]], [[2003]], [[Kami, Miyagi|Kami town]] was formed from a merger between three towns in the [[Kami District, Miyagi|Kami district]]; Miyazaki, Nakaniida, and Onoda.

*'''Higashimatsushima merger'''
On [[April 1]], [[2005]], [[Naruse, Miyagi|Naruse town]] and [[Yamoto, Miyagi|Yamoto town]] from the [[Monou District, Miyagi|Monou district]] merged and created the city of [[Higashimatsushima, Miyagi|Higashimatsushima]].  This merger, combined with the simultaneous Ishinomaki expansion, dissolved Monou district.

*'''Kurihara merger'''
On [[April 1]], [[2005]], all towns and villages in the [[Kurihara District, Miyagi|Kurihara district]] merged and created the city of [[Kurihara, Miyagi|Kurihara]].  One village and nine towns were involved in the merger.  They were [[Ichihasama, Miyagi|Ichihasama]], [[Kannari, Miyagi|Kannari]], [[Kurikoma, Miyagi|Kurikoma]], [[Semine, Miyagi|Semine]], [[Shiwahime, Miyagi|Shiwahime]], [[Takashimizu, Miyagi|Takashimizu]], [[Tsukidate, Miyagi|Tsukidate]], [[Wakayanagi, Miyagi|Wakayanagi]], along with [[Hanayama, Miyagi|Hanayama village]].

*'''Tome merger'''
On [[April 1]], [[2005]], all towns in the [[Tome District, Miyagi|Tome district]] merged with [[Tsuyama, Miyagi|Tsuyama town]] in the [[Motoyoshi District, Miyagi|Motoyoshi district]] and created the city of [[Tome, Miyagi|Tome]].  Nine towns merged, which were [[Hasama, Miyagi|Hasama]], [[Ishikoshi, Miyagi|Ishikoshi]], [[Minamikata, Miyagi|Minamikata]], [[Nakada, Miyagi|Nakada]], [[Tome, Miyagi|Tome]], [[Towa, Miyagi|Towa]], [[Toyosato, Miyagi|Toyosato]], [[Tsuyama, Miyagi|Tsuyama]], and [[Yoneyama, Miyagi|Yoneyama]]

*'''Ishinomaki expansion'''
On [[April 1]], [[2005]], five towns from the [[Monou District, Miyagi|Monou District]] and [[Oshika, Miyagi|Oshika]] town from the [[Oshika District, Miyagi|Oshika District]] merged into the city of [[Ishinomaki, Miyagi|Ishinomaki]].  This expansion, combined with the Higashimatsushima merger, dissolved Monou district.  The five towns from Monou district were [[Kahoku, Miyagi|Kahoku]], [[Kanan, Miyagi|Kanan]], [[Kitakami, Miyagi|Kitakami]], [[Monou, Miyagi|Monou]] and [[Ogatsu, Miyagi|Ogatsu]].

*'''Minamisanriku merger'''
On [[October 1]], [[2005]], the town of [[Shizugawa, Miyagi|Shizugawa]] and the town of [[Utatsu, Miyagi|Utatsu]] from the [[Motoyoshi District, Miyagi|Motoyoshi District]] merged to form the new town of [[Minamisanriku, Miyagi|Minamisanriku]]. [http://www.su-gappei.jp/ (Merger Information Page)]

*'''Misato expansion'''
On [[January 1]], [[2006]], the towns of [[Kogota, Miyagi|Kogota]] and [[Nangou, Miyagi|Nangou]] from [[Toda District, Miyagi|Toda District]] merged into the new town of  [[Misato, Miyagi|Misato]]. [http://www.town.kogota.miyagi.jp/gappei/ (Merger Information Page)]

===Future mergers===
*'''Kesennuma expansion'''
On [[March 31]], [[2006]], the town of [[Karakuwa, Miyagi|Karakuwa]] will merge into the city of  [[Kesennuma, Miyagi|Kesennuma]]. [http://www.k-macs.ne.jp/~kmk/ (Merger Information Page)]

== Economy ==

Although Miyagi has a good deal of fishing and agriculture, producing a great deal of rice and livestock, it is dominated by the manufacturing industries around Sendai, particularly electronics, appliances, and food processing.

== Demographics ==

== Culture ==

== Tourism ==

Sendai was the [[castle town]] of the [[daimyo]] [[Date Masamune]]. The remains of [[Sendai Castle]] stand on a hill above the city.

Miyagi Prefecture boasts one of Japan's three greatest sights. [[Matsushima]], the pine-clad islands, dot the waters off the coast of the prefecture.

